OpenH264's threads on a Unix that is neither Linux nor a BSD.

WelsThreadLib.cpp asks Linux with sched_getaffinity, and every other Unix
with sysctl(3) - "hw.logicalcpu" on Apple, "hw.ncpu" or "hw.ncpuonline" on
the BSDs - except Fuchsia, which has no sysctl and gets
sysconf(_SC_NPROCESSORS_ONLN). The list has no arm for a Unix in neither
family, so <sys/sysctl.h> is included unconditionally there and the file
stops.

It reaches this build because Electron's args turn proprietary_codecs on,
and media_use_openh264 follows it: the browser never compiled it.

Fuchsia's arm is the generic one - sysconf is what the others are standing
in for - so it is taken wherever the compiler says <sys/sysctl.h> is not
there, which names no operating system and changes nothing on any platform
that has one. The same shape as ANGLE's and Dawn's platform lists (0039,
0043): the library's own fallback, reached rather than written.

The same file creates its threads with pthread_attr_setscope(PTHREAD_SCOPE_
SYSTEM) and pthread_attr_setschedpolicy(SCHED_FIFO), except on Android and
Fuchsia. Both belong to POSIX's optional Thread Execution Scheduling option,
and a C library without it does not define PTHREAD_SCOPE_SYSTEM - this one
does not, because its scheduler has two priority classes and no real-time
policy, and accepting SCHED_FIFO would be the stored-and-ignored setting
M65 refuses. Neither call changes anything where it does compile: a policy
set on an attribute with the default PTHREAD_INHERIT_SCHED is not used.
So the calls are asked of the header, by the macro the option defines.

diff --git a/third_party/openh264/src/codec/common/src/WelsThreadLib.cpp b/third_party/openh264/src/codec/common/src/WelsThreadLib.cpp
